  ],
  "text": "Your message crosses an ocean as flashes of light bouncing inside a strand thinner than a hair. You picture the internet as radio, or as a cloud. It is mostly glass, lying in the dark. Shine light into glass at a shallow enough angle and it cannot get back out. It reaches the boundary, reflects straight back in, and does it again. Every single time. So the light never travels straight. It ricochets the whole length of the fibre. And the glass is made so pure that the signal survives a hundred kilometres before it needs a boost. Each strand carries terabits a second, and one cable holds a bundle of them. There are hundreds of those cables on the seafloor, right now, carrying almost everything. The cloud is not in the sky. It is a rope of glass in the dark at the bottom of the ocean. Every message you have ever sent abroad went as light, trapped in a thread it could not escape. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
